<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-12-markov">
    <title>12 - Markov Chain Models</title>

    <!-- Optional introduction -->
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this activity we are going to practice modeling with Markov
            chains.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <!-- Exercises start here. -->
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the Xerox copier in the math department office.
                There are two possible states: working and broken.
                You are given the following assumptions: If currently working,
                there is a 98% chance the copier is working the next day.
                If currently broken, there is a 30% probability that it is
                working the next day.
            </p>

            <p>
                Make sure to import the necessary libraries!
            </p>

            <program language="python">
                <code>
import math
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from numpy import linalg as LA # NEW!
                </code>
            </program>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Take the probability transition matrix you created for this
                    scenario in the class preparation assignment and code it
                    into Python.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <em>Remember, we can create a matrix using <c>np.array([[1,0],[0,1]])</c>
                    (the identity matrix as an example). </em>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Code a vector for the initial state of being working.
                    This vector should be
                    <m>{\vec{x} = \left[\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ 0 \\ \end{array}\right].}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the condition after 1 day, 10 days, and 100 days by
                    using matrix-vector multiplication.
                    Hint: consider raising the matrix to the corresponding
                    power.
                </p>

                <p>
                    In Python, we can do matrix-vector multiplication by doing
                    <c>A@x0</c> and raising a matrix to a power by
                    <c>LA.matrix_power(A,100)</c>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Plot iterations from 0 to 200 days.
                    Do this as a scatter plot with two curves.
                    The y-component will be either working or broken with the
                    x-component corresponding to the iteration/day.
                    What do you notice for the long term behavior?
                    How does this compare to the value you found at Day 100?
                </p>

                <p>
                    Remember, in Python a scatter plot is made using
                    <c>plt.scatter()</c>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now, find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors.
                    Do the eigenvalues follow the Perron-Frobenius Theorem?
                    If yes, find the corresponding steady-state vector.
                    <em>
                    Note: an eigenvector might not have entries that sum
                    to 1, but the steady-state vector must. Make sure to
                    modify the vector as needed to take care of this!</em>
                    How does this steady-state vector compare to the long term
                    behavior you found previously?
                </p>

                <p>
                    Some helpful coding commands to consider:
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <c>u, v = LA.eig(A)</c> to find the eigenvalues u,
                            and corresponding eigenvectors v
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            If your vector is called vec, you can find the sum
                            of the elements by <c>vec.sum()</c>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider a random walk on the following graph of four vertices.
                Here, the edge weights give the probability of going from one
                vertex to another.
            </p>

            <image source="images/MarkovChainDiagram.png" width="50%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Random walk Markov chain graph.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Make the probability transition matrix.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Does this model have a steady-state that represents long
                    term behavior?
                    Explain.
                    If yes, find it!
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider a random walk on the following graph of five vertices.
                Here, the edge weights give the probability of going from one
                vertex to another.
            </p>

            <image source="images/Markov2.png" width="45%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Random walk Markov chain graph.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Make the probability transition matrix if the probability of
                    going from vertex <m>u</m> to vertex <m>v</m> is given by
                    <m>\frac{1}{d_u}</m> if the edge from <m>u</m> to <m>v</m>
                    exists and 0 otherwise.
                    Here <m>d_u</m> is the degree of <m>u</m>, i.e., how many
                    edges stem from that vertex.
                    For example, <m>d_2 = 3.</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Does this model have a steady-state that represents long
                    term behavior?
                    Explain.
                    If yes, find it!
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If you'd like to explore more, Markov established Markov chains
                while analyzing the vowel and consonant patterns with a text
                called <em>Eugene Onegin</em>.
                See the link on Blackboard to read more!
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>